COMPUTE LEDGER - Issue 34 - Monday, March 2, 2026

Three things this week.

1. Tianhe Research releases a 200B-parameter mixture-of-experts model under Apache 2.0

Sixteen experts, two active per token, so it serves like a 30B model and reasons like something much bigger. It is the strongest open model on our multilingual and code suites and within a point of the closed frontier on long-context retrieval. The US hosting providers had it up within 36 hours. Whether enterprises will run a model from a Shenzhen lab in production is a procurement question, not a technical one; two of the three enterprises we asked said their security teams have already said no.

2. We benchmarked the four major serving frameworks so you do not have to

Same hardware, same model, same traffic replay from a real customer-support workload. The spread in cost per million output tokens was 2.3x between the best and worst configurations, and most of the gap was default settings: batch size, KV-cache quantization, and whether prefix caching was on. The full results and configs are on the site. The takeaway is boring and expensive: a week of tuning pays for itself in a month.

3. The interconnection queue is the new GPU queue

Utilities in Virginia, Texas and Arizona now quote five to seven years for new large-load interconnections. Data-center developers are responding with on-site gas turbines, which are themselves on a three-year lead time. The practical effect for anyone renting compute is that the capacity coming online in 2026 was permitted in 2022, and there is not much of it.

That is the issue. Reply with tips; we read everything.

Compute Ledger is written by two people in Oakland and supported by readers. Forward it to someone who runs a GPU budget.
